Below is a quick‑start guide you can copy‑paste into your mapping software (or a simple HTML page) to display the map you’ve been building – the polygon overlay and the three custom markers.

---

## 1️⃣  Create (or reuse) a Google Maps embed

```html
<!-- 1️⃣  Embed a base map (you can change the center, zoom, size, etc.) -->
<iframe
    id="globalMap"
    width="800"               <!-- map width  -->
    height="600"              <!-- map height -->
    style="border:0"
    loading="lazy"
    allowfullscreen
    src="https://www.google.com/maps/embed/v1/view
        ?key=YOUR_GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY          <!-- ← replace with your API key -->
        &center=48.8566,2.3522                 <!-- center of the map (Paris) -->
        &zoom=5                                 <!-- zoom level (adjust as needed) -->
        &maptype=roadmap">
</iframe>
```

*Replace `YOUR_GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY` with the key you use for your own Google Maps integration.*  
If you already have a map container in your software, just set its `src` to the URL above.

---

## 2️⃣  Add the polygon overlay (the two‑point polygon you defined)

If your software lets you add GeoJSON layers, paste the following GeoJSON object.  
It draws a semi‑transparent polygon that connects the two points you supplied.

```json
{
  "type": "FeatureCollection",
  "features": [
    {
      "type": "Feature",
      "properties": {
        "stroke": "#FF0000",          // optional outline colour
        "stroke-width": 2,
        "fill": "#FF0000",            // fill colour
        "fill-opacity": 0.5           // 50 % transparency
      },
      "geometry": {
        "type": "Polygon",
        "coordinates": [
          [
            [2.3522, 48.8566],   // (lon, lat) – Paris
            [12.4964, 41.9028]   // (lon, lat) – Rome
          ]
        ]
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

*How to add it*  
- **Web‑based GIS / Leaflet / OpenLayers** – use `L.geoJSON(yourGeoJSON).addTo(map);` or the equivalent.  
- **Google Maps JavaScript API** – create a `google.maps.Data()` layer, call `loadGeoJson(yourGeoJSON)` and set the style to respect `fillOpacity: 0.5`.  
- **Desktop GIS (QGIS, ArcGIS)** – import the JSON as a “GeoJSON” layer and set the layer’s fill opacity to 50 %.

---

## 3️⃣  Add the three custom markers

Below is a tiny JavaScript snippet (for a web page using the Google Maps JavaScript API) that adds the three markers you asked for. Adjust the `map` variable to point at the map instance you created in step 1.

```js
// Assuming you already have a `map` object (Google Maps JavaScript API)
const markers = [
  {
    position: { lat: 48.8566, lng: 2.3522 },
    title: "Statue of Liberty - Northwest"
  },
  {
    position: { lat: 41.9028, lng: 12.4964 },
    title: "Statue of Liberty - Southeast"
  },
  {
    position: { lat: 37.7749, lng: 122.4194 },
    title: "San Francisco"
  }
];

markers.forEach(m => {
  new google.maps.Marker({
    position: m.position,
    map: map,
    title: m.title
  });
});
```

*If you’re not using the Google Maps JavaScript API* – most mapping SDKs (Leaflet, Mapbox GL, OpenLayers, etc.) have a similar “add marker” call; just feed the same latitude/longitude and title values.

---

## 4️⃣  Putting it all together (full HTML example)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <title>GlobalMap – Custom Overlay & Markers</title>
  <style>#globalMap { width: 800px; height: 600px; }</style>
  <script src="https://maps.googleapis.com/maps/api/js?key=YOUR_GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY"></script>
</head>
<body>

<div id="globalMap"></div>

<script>
  // 1️⃣  Initialise the base map
  const map = new google.maps.Map(document.getElementById('globalMap'), {
    center: { lat: 48.8566, lng: 2.3522 }, // Paris
    zoom: 5,
    mapTypeId: 'roadmap'
  });

  // 2️⃣  Load the polygon (GeoJSON)
  const geoJson = {
    "type": "FeatureCollection",
    "features": [
      {
        "type": "Feature",
        "properties": {
          "stroke": "#FF0000",
          "stroke-width": 2,
          "fill": "#FF0000",
          "fill-opacity": 0.5
        },
        "geometry": {
          "type": "Polygon",
          "coordinates": [
            [
              [2.3522, 48.8566],
              [12.4964, 41.9028]
            ]
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  };
  map.data.addGeoJson(geoJson);
  map.data.setStyle({
    fillColor: '#FF0000',
    fillOpacity: 0.5,
    strokeColor: '#FF0000',
    strokeWeight: 2
  });

  // 3️⃣  Add the three markers
  const markers = [
    { lat: 48.8566, lng: 2.3522, title: "Statue of Liberty - Northwest" },
    { lat: 41.9028, lng: 12.4964, title: "Statue of Liberty - Southeast" },
    { lat: 37.7749, lng: 122.4194, title: "San Francisco" }
  ];
  markers.forEach(m => {
    new google.maps.Marker({
      position: { lat: m.lat, lng: m.lng },
      map: map,
      title: m.title
    });
  });
</script>

</body>
</html>
```

*Save the file as `globalMap.html`, replace `YOUR_GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY` with your own key, and open it in a browser. The map will show the semi‑transparent polygon and the three labeled markers.*

---

## 5️⃣  How to import into other mapping platforms

| Platform | How to add the overlay & markers |
|----------|-----------------------------------|
| **Leaflet** | Use `L.geoJSON(geoJson).addTo(map);` and `L.marker([lat, lng], {title: "..."}).addTo(map);` |
| **Mapbox GL JS** | Add the GeoJSON as a source (`map.addSource('polygon', {type: 'geojson', data: geoJson});`) then a fill layer with `fill-opacity: 0.5`. Add markers with `new mapboxgl.Marker().setLngLat([lng, lat]).setPopup(new mapboxgl.Popup().setText('title')).addTo(map);` |
| **ArcGIS Online / Web AppBuilder** | Upload the GeoJSON as a feature layer, set the renderer’s fill opacity to 50 %, and add point graphics for the three locations. |
| **QGIS** | `Layer → Add Layer → Add Vector Layer…` → select the GeoJSON file. Set the layer’s symbology to 50 % opacity. Add three “point” layers (or a single point layer) with the given coordinates and titles. |
| **Desktop software (e.g., Google Earth Pro)** | Save the GeoJSON as a `.kml`/`.kmz` (many converters exist) and import it. Then add placemarks for the three points. |

---

### TL;DR

1. **Embed a base map** (use the iframe or JavaScript API).  
2. **Add the polygon** using the GeoJSON snippet (opacity = 0.5).  
3. **Add the three markers** with the exact lat/long and titles you supplied.  

If you need a different map centre, zoom level, or a different size, just tweak the `center`, `zoom`, `width`, and `height` values in the examples above. Let me know if you run into any hiccups or need the code adapted for a specific SDK!

